hatari — the emulator
    GNU General Public License, version 2 or later. Hatari's own readme states
    that linking it statically or dynamically makes a combined work, which is one
    reason PiST runs it as a separate process.
    https://www.hatari-emu.org/

    Redistribution: release archives of PiST *do* include a Hatari binary,
    compiled by the release workflow from the upstream, unmodified source
    tarball, pinned by checksum. The basis for this is the licence above: the
    GPL, version 2 or later, permits redistribution of the compiled binary.
    The binary itself is conveyed under version 2 rather than "or later",
    because three of the files Hatari compiles in grant version 2 alone — the
    "or later" option on the other files cannot lift the combined work. See
    the readline entry below, and docs/PLAN.md §10.

    Two commitments follow from that basis:
      - the binary is NEVER patched or modified. PiST drives Hatari purely
        through command-line arguments.
      - it is NEVER committed to the PiST source repository. It is produced by
        the release workflow at release time only.

    Version and source: the hrdb-main fork of Hatari — upstream 2.6.1 plus the
    remote-debug listener PiST's HRDB transport uses — built from the
    unmodified source at the pinned commit
    https://github.com/tattlemuss/hatari/tree/21aa4cb76783eb1b141b917fa1976c9c01331d66
    whose tarball is pinned by SHA-256
    61be3f026ff6e930a5bdf7fd25e5b755126ec678cea0c483d93a71f916be7a05
    It is built with SDL2 (zlib licence) and libpng (libpng licence).
    (PiST's debug transport selects itself from the emulator binary's content,
    so the bundle carries exactly one emulator: this one.)

    Bundling is necessary because no distribution package is a usable version.
    Ubuntu 22.04 ships 2.3.1 and Ubuntu 24.04 ships 2.4.1, while PiST is
    developed and verified against 2.6.1 — and 2.4.1 returns truncated debugger
    responses that break source-line debugging, so relying on the user's package
    manager cannot work.

    This applies to the Linux AppImage and the Windows archive. The Windows
    build is compiled with MSYS2 ucrt64 from the same pinned commit and ships
    its runtime DLLs (SDL2, libpng, zlib, the GCC runtime) beside the exe. The
    macOS archive still requires the user to install Hatari themselves
    (`brew install hatari` carries 2.6.1).

readline — NOT redistributed
    GNU General Public License, version 3 or later.
    https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/readline/

    PiST does not ship it, and the bundled Hatari is built without it: no
    readline library travels in any archive, and no archive's generated
    THIRD-PARTY.txt lists readline as a component. (The AppImage still carries
    a copy of GPL-3.0.txt, but for Qt — the LGPLv3 incorporates the GPLv3 by
    reference — not for readline.)

    This is a licence constraint, not a packaging preference. Hatari is
    *mostly* licensed version 2 or later, and version 3 code joins version 2
    code only through that "or later" option — which is exactly the argument
    that used to be made here for carrying readline. It does not hold. Three
    files Hatari compiles into every binary — src/cpu/uae/attributes.h,
    src/cpu/uae/types.h and src/cpu/uae/vm.h — grant version 2 alone, with no
    "or later", and a licence is not upgraded by majority vote: the Hatari
    binaries this project conveys are therefore GPLv2 as combined works, and a
    GPL version 3 library cannot join them. docs/PLAN.md §10 audits those three
    files and the include chains that reach them.

    Nothing is lost functionally. Without readline Hatari's debugger falls back
    to `fgets` and writes its prompt to *stderr* rather than to stdout. PiST
    frames a command on the `> ` prompt on either stream — it counts `> ` on
    stdout and stderr — so the transport works the same way built either way,
    and docs/PLAN.md §2.4 records which configurations the suites run against.

A TOS ROM image is also required. Original Atari TOS images remain copyrighted
and are NEVER distributed with PiST; the user supplies one. The ROM's version is
read from the image header, and TOS 1.04 or later is required for the
GEMDOS-hard-disk run path.

EmuTOS is bundled instead, so that a fresh install runs:
    EmuTOS 1.4, the 1024k emulator image (etos1024k.img),
    GNU General Public License version 2.
    One image covers ST through Falcon and is the same build Hatari ships.
    Verified to autostart a program from the GEMDOS hard disk, which is what the
    Run and debug path requires.
    Source: https://emutos.sourceforge.net/


Build and link dependencies
---------------------------

Qt 6 (Core, Gui, Widgets, Network, Test)
    Available under the GNU LGPL version 3, GPL version 2 or 3, or a commercial
    licence from The Qt Company. PiST uses it under the LGPLv3 and links Qt
    *dynamically*, which keeps the LGPL relink obligation satisfiable: a user who
    replaces the Qt libraries can still relink and run PiST. The complete
    corresponding source of the Qt version used must be made available, even
    unmodified.
    https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/licensing.html


No code from Hatari, vasm, vlink or Qt is included in this repository's sources.

The ST colour-word layout, Degas/NeoChrome/IFF/STOS bitplane packing, and paint-tool geometry in
`src/image/` were reimplemented from the known file formats and from the behaviour of the
LemonAndLime sprite editor (a separate project; not vendored, not linked).

The flood-fill, palette, brush, eyedropper, undo, and selection toolbar glyphs follow the
silhouettes of the SVG Repo paint-bucket, paint-palette, paint-brush, dropper-2,
undo-left-round, and selection-plus-duotone icons in
docs/paint-bucket-fill-svgrepo-com.svg, docs/paint-palette-svgrepo-com.svg,
docs/paint-brush-svgrepo-com.svg, docs/dropper-2-svgrepo-com.svg,
docs/undo-left-round-svgrepo-com.svg, and docs/selection-plus-duotone-svgrepo-com.svg;
they are redrawn as themed QPainterPaths rather
than loading those files at runtime (the redo glyph mirrors the undo one).


Bundled licence texts
---------------------

Every release archive carries the licence texts of what is actually inside it
in share/doc/pist/licenses/, with a manifest (share/doc/pist/THIRD-PARTY.txt)
generated from the archive's contents by packaging/collect-notices.py — the
binaries present and the libraries the bundled Hatari links. The set is
derived, not declared: whether Hatari links Capstone depends on the build
host, so Capstone's BSD-3-Clause text ships exactly when Capstone does, and a
component that appears without its text fails the release build.
